id: pm-ghgf4a
title: "Secret guard: keep lexer distrust after a declined quote local to the affected segment, and decode adjacent Python literals before judging a value"
description: "pm-gh1781 made the string lexer conservative: once a line declines a quote of a kind (a quote right after a word character), no later string of that kind on the line ends a value, because a shell segment opened there shifts every later boundary and a cut there hid literals main reported. The rule over-reaches in valid structured text: a plain YAML flow scalar holding a prose apostrophe disables every later single-quoted boundary on its line, and a shell word with a joined double-quoted segment does the same for double quotes, so a later member holding only a bare reference is judged as literal text; main reported nothing for both. Separately, the shell-word text reading of a single-quoted value joins Python adjacent literals but keeps a hex escape between them literal, so a value Python decodes to exactly one bare reference is reported. Found by the Codex round 5 review of PR #1789; both are false positives, no lost finding."
type: Issue
status: open
priority: 3
tags[3]: "area:security",false-positive,secret-guard
created_at: "2026-11-26T17:33:54.789Z"
updated_at: "2026-11-26T18:56:46.921Z"
author: "harness:claude-code"
estimated_minutes: 180
acceptance_criteria: "After a declined quote, trust resumes at a boundary that resolves the ambiguity (a confirmed scalar, flow-mapping or shell-word boundary), so a later member's bare reference is clean while the shell desync lines from pm-gh1781 stay reported; Adjacent Python string literals are decoded and joined before the value is judged in Python expression context; the backslash-keeping join stays for shell words; The pm-gh1781 corpus differential shows no lost finding"
parent: pm-qwoy
risk: medium
confidence: medium
expected_result: A bare reference in a structured member is a placeholder however the line began
actual_result: A prose apostrophe or a joined shell segment earlier on the line makes a later bare reference a finding
dependencies[1]{id,kind,created_at,author,source_kind,author_source}:
  pm-gh1781,discovered_from,"2026-11-26T17:33:54.789Z","harness:claude-code","cli:create:dep",detected
notes[3]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-11-26T18:08:47.054Z","harness:claude-code","Also in scope, from Greptile and Codex round 5 on PR #1789: a Python string that escapes an apostrophe (an escaped-apostrophe bytes or raw string, or an unprefixed one after an assignment) reads as a shell close under the per-line lexer, so a later dict member on its line keeps the conservative reading and a bare reference there is reported. Two narrower exceptions (treating the quote that ends the word after an escaped apostrophe as Python's closer, and judging a prefixed string's escapes by the context before its prefix) were tried and reverted on PR #1789, because each let a shell line hide a literal that main reports (both lines are spec cases). A fix needs a signal that separates Python from shell context beyond one quote's neighbourhood."
  "2026-11-26T18:45:36.383Z","harness:claude-code","Also in scope, from Codex round 6 on PR #1789: when a cut is withheld on an ambiguous line, the accepted flat match can consume the next YAML flow member, so a later member's own assignment is not scanned separately and the finding is reported at the earlier placeholder's offset. A finding still fires; a bounded scan of later member keys after a withheld cut would report it at the literal."
  "2026-11-26T18:56:46.920Z","harness:claude-code","Also in scope, from Codex rounds 6 and 7 on PR #1789: outside every string a backslash before a quote is a shell escape but a literal path separator to PowerShell and cmd.exe, so the lexer now marks such a line ambiguous; a PowerShell line whose quoted value is only a reference, and a shell line with an escaped quote before a quoted member, keep the conservative reading. Dialect-aware rules (when the text's shell is known) would clear both without letting the PowerShell desync line, which main misses, hide its literal again."
body: ""
